From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 21:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375: correct the size of the removed
 region

The removed region on SM6375 is 0x7100000, not 0x5100000. Linux is
currently handing 32 MB of firmware-owned memory (0xc5100000-0xc7100000)
out as ordinary RAM.

The value in mainline matches shima and yupik. It does not match this
SoC. From the vendor tree (Motorola-SM6375-Devs/android_kernel_motorola_sm6375,
branch fifteen), arch/arm64/boot/dts/vendor/qcom:

    blair.dtsi   (SM6375, this SoC)  reg = <0x0 0xc0000000 0x0 0x7100000>
    holi.dtsi    (sibling)           reg = <0x0 0xc0000000 0x0 0x7100000>
    shima.dtsi                       reg = <0x0 0xc0000000 0x0 0x5100000>
    yupik.dtsi                       reg = <0x0 0xc0000000 0x0 0x5100000>

rhodep is a blair board: blair-moto-rhodep-base.dts includes blair.dtsi.

Nothing in the device tree references removed_mem; it is a pure no-map
reservation for the secure firmware, so an undersized reservation
produces no probe failure, no bus abort and no log message. It gives
Linux 32 MB that TrustZone believes it owns, and the corruption that
follows is silent by construction.

Every other fixed reservation in mainline's sm6375.dtsi was checked
against blair.dtsi and they all match: hyp, xbl_aop, reserved_xbl_uefi,
smem, fw, pil_wlan, pil_adsp, pil_cdsp, pil_video, pil_ipa_fw,
pil_ipa_gsi, pil_gpu_micro_code, pil_mpss_wlan and the splash/dfps pair.
This is the only one that differs.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
@@ -612,7 +612,7 @@
 		};
 
 		removed_mem: removed@c0000000 {
-			reg = <0 0xc0000000 0 0x5100000>;
+			reg = <0 0xc0000000 0 0x7100000>;
 			no-map;
 		};
 
